[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 1: Lines With 4...d5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/a700329e-dc5f-4868-8f40-3cd568d80858/studies/71c57242-121e-40b7-9875-80cf441d9dcd/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian Defense. And now we play c3. The main idea is to play d4. In addition, we want to get Black out of their theory!} 2.c3 2...Nc6 {Nc6, developing, is the most popular move. It is played 35% of times. It covers the d4 square somewhat, but we can still push with d4. And we will!} 3.d4 3...cxd4 {Black usually plays this trade. So let's just recapture. Now we have two strong central pawns! Not bad!} 4.cxd4 4...d5 {This is the most popular move, being played over 2 million times. Black wants to challenge our central pawns. We'll just take here, and gain some tempi on the queen.} 5.exd5 5...Qxd5 {Black can recapture and attack the d4-pawn. Black has two developed pieces, so they are quite active. Before we gain a tempo, we have to defend the d-pawn.} 6.Nf3 6...Bg4 {This pin is actually quite challenging. We'll break the pin and defend.} 7.Be2 7...e6 {Since 7...Bxf3 achieves nothing, 7...e6 for normal development is best. We should attack the queen here with Nc3. Black has a few options.} 8.Nc3 8...Qa5 {The queen on a5 pins the knight. Now h3 is a useful move to see if Black bites and takes on f3.} 9.h3 9...Bh5 {They usually do not do so. But d5 is free here. You shouldn't be concerned about the d-file.} 10.d5 10...Rd8 {Placing the rook on the d-file to pin the pawn is tricky. However, our idea is development plus the basic Nd4 idea for the check on e2. First we will ensure our king safety. Then we can go for tactics.} 11.O-O 11...exd5 {After Black takes, we don't care about a pawn. Nd4 opens up lines. We want to focus on the king. 12...Bg6 fails due to 13.Bb5 and suddenly that e-file is cleared for the rook!} 12.Nd4 12...Bxe2 {This is the best move, but it allows us e-file play. We'll take back with check.} 13.Qxe2+ {Check!} 13...Be7 {After the bishop blocks, Black has the same problems as in the main line. We will increase pressure with Nb5, Re1 and Bg5. White is better.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 1: Lines With 4...d5 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/a700329e-dc5f-4868-8f40-3cd568d80858/studies/71c57242-121e-40b7-9875-80cf441d9dcd/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian Defense. And now we play c3. The main idea is to play d4. In addition, we want to get Black out of their theory!} 2.c3 2...Nc6 {Nc6, developing, is the most popular move. It is played 35% of times. It covers the d4 square somewhat, but we can still push with d4. And we will!} 3.d4 3...cxd4 {Black usually plays this trade. So let's just recapture. Now we have two strong central pawns! Not bad!} 4.cxd4 4...d5 {This is the most popular move, being played over 2 million times. Black wants to challenge our central pawns. We'll just take here, and gain some tempi on the queen.} 5.exd5 5...Qxd5 {Black can recapture and attack the d4-pawn. Black has two developed pieces, so they are quite active. Before we gain a tempo, we have to defend the d-pawn.} 6.Nf3 6...e5 {This is a risky way for Black to play. I like attacking the queen while developing here.} 7.Nc3 7...Bb4 {This pin is the only move that doesn't lose. But after we unpin, Black will have to give us the bishop pair.} 8.Bd2 8...Bxc3 {The bishop takes. We'll recapture with the bishop. This is the most active move.} 9.Bxc3 9...e4 {This continuation is quite common and logical. But logical and straightforward continuations aren't necessarily okay. We'll push the knight into e5 and play for the advantage.} 10.Ne5 10...Nxe5 {This capture, for example, is quite natural. But it's bad! After we recapture, the e5-pawn is a beast on that square.} 11.dxe5 11...Qxd1+ {Another natural move.} 12.Rxd1 {But we control the d-file and the d6- and f6-squares. Bc4 and Rd6 are great ways to continue, and a great plan is to castle and place the rook on e1 to attack e4. White is again borderline winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 1: Lines With 4...d5 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/a700329e-dc5f-4868-8f40-3cd568d80858/studies/71c57242-121e-40b7-9875-80cf441d9dcd/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian Defense. And now we play c3. The main idea is to play d4. In addition, we want to get Black out of their theory!} 2.c3 2...Nc6 {Nc6, developing, is the most popular move. It is played 35% of times. It covers the d4 square somewhat, but we can still push with d4. And we will!} 3.d4 3...cxd4 {Black usually plays this trade. So let's just recapture. Now we have two strong central pawns! Not bad!} 4.cxd4 4...d5 {This is the most popular move, being played over 2 million times. Black wants to challenge our central pawns. We'll just take here, and gain some tempi on the queen.} 5.exd5 5...Qxd5 {Black can recapture and attack the d4-pawn. Black has two developed pieces, so they are quite active. Before we gain a tempo, we have to defend the d-pawn.} 6.Nf3 6...Nf6 {To show you how things can go wrong for Black, even natural development is bad.} 7.Nc3 {d5 is coming. Black is losing. d5, Bf4, Nb5. This should be easy.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 1: Lines With 4...d5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/a700329e-dc5f-4868-8f40-3cd568d80858/studies/71c57242-121e-40b7-9875-80cf441d9dcd/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian Defense. And now we play c3. The main idea is to play d4. In addition, we want to get Black out of their theory!} 2.c3 2...Nc6 {Nc6, developing, is the most popular move. It is played 35% of times. It covers the d4 square somewhat, but we can still push with d4. And we will!} 3.d4 3...cxd4 {Black usually plays this trade. So let's just recapture. Now we have two strong central pawns! Not bad!} 4.cxd4 4...d5 {This is the most popular move, being played over 2 million times. Black wants to challenge our central pawns. We'll just take here, and gain some tempi on the queen.} 5.exd5 5...Qxd5 {Black can recapture and attack the d4-pawn. Black has two developed pieces, so they are quite active. Before we gain a tempo, we have to defend the d-pawn.} 6.Nf3 6...Bg4 {This pin is actually quite challenging. We'll break the pin and defend.} 7.Be2 7...e6 {Since 7...Bxf3 achieves nothing, 7...e6 for normal development is best. We should attack the queen here with Nc3. Black has a few options.} 8.Nc3 8...Bb4 {This one is by far the most popular. Black pins the knight. Castling here rekindles the threat of Nxd5.} 9.O-O 9...Bxc3 {Black doesn't have to take the knight, but they often do. This trade favors us because we no longer have an IQP after recapturing.} 10.bxc3 10...Nf6 {After 10...Nf6, Black may think they're ready to castle.} 11.Ba3 {But we can control the e7- and f8-squares. We'll also be attacking Black if they castle on the queenside. Moves like Rb1 and Qb3 (if Black moves their queen away from d5) will be great for b-file pressure.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 1: Lines With 4...d5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/a700329e-dc5f-4868-8f40-3cd568d80858/studies/71c57242-121e-40b7-9875-80cf441d9dcd/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian Defense. And now we play c3. The main idea is to play d4. In addition, we want to get Black out of their theory!} 2.c3 2...Nc6 {Nc6, developing, is the most popular move. It is played 35% of times. It covers the d4 square somewhat, but we can still push with d4. And we will!} 3.d4 3...cxd4 {Black usually plays this trade. So let's just recapture. Now we have two strong central pawns! Not bad!} 4.cxd4 4...d5 {This is the most popular move, being played over 2 million times. Black wants to challenge our central pawns. We'll just take here, and gain some tempi on the queen.} 5.exd5 5...Qxd5 {Black can recapture and attack the d4-pawn. Black has two developed pieces, so they are quite active. Before we gain a tempo, we have to defend the d-pawn.} 6.Nf3 6...Bg4 {This pin is actually quite challenging. We'll break the pin and defend.} 7.Be2 7...e6 {Since 7...Bxf3 achieves nothing, 7...e6 for normal development is best. We should attack the queen here with Nc3. Black has a few options.} 8.Nc3 8...Bb4 {This one is by far the most popular. Black pins the knight. Castling here rekindles the threat of Nxd5.} 9.O-O 9...Bxc3 {Black doesn't have to take the knight, but they often do. This trade favors us because we no longer have an IQP after recapturing.} 10.bxc3 10...Nge7 {To cover those dark squares, many players will get their knight on e7. Ba3 doesn't prevent ...0-0 anymore. But the b-file is still weak and White has a semi-open file to attack on.} 11.Rb1 11...b6 {Black usually plays their pawn up. But we can begin attacking the queen, with c4.} 12.c4 12...Qd7 {The queen goes to d7. And Ba3 is still a great move here. If Black castles, they'll be in a pin.} 13.Ba3 13...O-O {So if Black castles for instance, d5 wins.} 14.d5 {There isn't a good way to deal with the pressure. If the knight moves to a5, Ne5 comes. The engine even thinks that giving up the exchange is the best idea by going for mass trades on d5. All of this thanks to a more mobile center thanks to our early 2. c3 move.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 1: Lines With 4...d5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/a700329e-dc5f-4868-8f40-3cd568d80858/studies/71c57242-121e-40b7-9875-80cf441d9dcd/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian Defense. And now we play c3. The main idea is to play d4. In addition, we want to get Black out of their theory!} 2.c3 2...Nc6 {Nc6, developing, is the most popular move. It is played 35% of times. It covers the d4 square somewhat, but we can still push with d4. And we will!} 3.d4 3...cxd4 {Black usually plays this trade. So let's just recapture. Now we have two strong central pawns! Not bad!} 4.cxd4 4...d5 {This is the most popular move, being played over 2 million times. Black wants to challenge our central pawns. We'll just take here, and gain some tempi on the queen.} 5.exd5 5...Qxd5 {Black can recapture and attack the d4-pawn. Black has two developed pieces, so they are quite active. Before we gain a tempo, we have to defend the d-pawn.} 6.Nf3 6...Bg4 {This pin is actually quite challenging. We'll break the pin and defend.} 7.Be2 7...e6 {Since 7...Bxf3 achieves nothing, 7...e6 for normal development is best. We should attack the queen here with Nc3. Black has a few options.} 8.Nc3 8...Qd8 {I suppose many players could go for this, which reminds me of a Qd8 Scandinavian. We'll kick the bishop back. White enjoys a stable edge and may even play for d5 to get rid of the IQP.} 9.h3 9...Bh5 {The bishop goes to h5.} 10.Qb3 {And d5 is pretty much guaranteed. White will be pretty comfortable in a middlegame if this move is played because they'll have virtually zero weaknesses. Black on the other hand is still down in development.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 1: Lines With 4...d5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/a700329e-dc5f-4868-8f40-3cd568d80858/studies/71c57242-121e-40b7-9875-80cf441d9dcd/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian Defense. And now we play c3. The main idea is to play d4. In addition, we want to get Black out of their theory!} 2.c3 2...Nc6 {Nc6, developing, is the most popular move. It is played 35% of times. It covers the d4 square somewhat, but we can still push with d4. And we will!} 3.d4 3...cxd4 {Black usually plays this trade. So let's just recapture. Now we have two strong central pawns! Not bad!} 4.cxd4 4...d5 {This is the most popular move, being played over 2 million times. Black wants to challenge our central pawns. We'll just take here, and gain some tempi on the queen.} 5.exd5 5...Qxd5 {Black can recapture and attack the d4-pawn. Black has two developed pieces, so they are quite active. Before we gain a tempo, we have to defend the d-pawn.} 6.Nf3 6...Bg4 {This pin is actually quite challenging. We'll break the pin and defend.} 7.Be2 7...e6 {Since 7...Bxf3 achieves nothing, 7...e6 for normal development is best. We should attack the queen here with Nc3. Black has a few options.} 8.Nc3 8...Qa5 {The queen on a5 pins the knight. Now h3 is a useful move to see if Black bites and takes on f3.} 9.h3 9...Bh5 {They usually do not do so. But d5 is free here. You shouldn't be concerned about the d-file.} 10.d5 10...exd5 {If Black takes, this is exactly what we wanted. The main idea behind d5 is not to recapture right away. We want to play this discovered attack.} 11.Nd4 11...Bxe2 {Black captures on e2, but in doing so, they bring our queen to the open file.} 12.Qxe2+ 12...Be7 {The bishop blocks, but this is a pin. Just taking is fine here. We remove a defender of the e7-bishop.} 13.Nxc6 13...bxc6 {Black recaptures. We'll castle. Our rook is coming to e1.} 14.O-O 14...Nf6 {The nice thing about taking on c6 is that once the g8-knight comes out, only the king defends e7.} 15.Bg5 {Black's best move is 15...Kf8. We have won the opening battle and have denied Black the luxury of castling.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 1: Lines With 4...d5 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/a700329e-dc5f-4868-8f40-3cd568d80858/studies/71c57242-121e-40b7-9875-80cf441d9dcd/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian Defense. And now we play c3. The main idea is to play d4. In addition, we want to get Black out of their theory!} 2.c3 2...Nc6 {Nc6, developing, is the most popular move. It is played 35% of times. It covers the d4 square somewhat, but we can still push with d4. And we will!} 3.d4 3...cxd4 {Black usually plays this trade. So let's just recapture. Now we have two strong central pawns! Not bad!} 4.cxd4 4...d5 {This is the most popular move, being played over 2 million times. Black wants to challenge our central pawns. We'll just take here, and gain some tempi on the queen.} 5.exd5 5...Qxd5 {Black can recapture and attack the d4-pawn. Black has two developed pieces, so they are quite active. Before we gain a tempo, we have to defend the d-pawn.} 6.Nf3 6...Bg4 {This pin is actually quite challenging. We'll break the pin and defend.} 7.Be2 7...Bxf3 {This is not how chess works. We get a free tempo when we recapture.} 8.Bxf3 8...Qxd4 {This blunder runs into the tactic of removing the defender. Black should retreat and accept they've miscalculated. We'll take on c6 with check.} 9.Bxc6+ 9...bxc6 {Without the knight's defense, the queen is hanging.} 10.Qxd4 {Black should not play 7...Bxf3!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 1: Lines With 4...d5 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/a700329e-dc5f-4868-8f40-3cd568d80858/studies/71c57242-121e-40b7-9875-80cf441d9dcd/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian Defense. And now we play c3. The main idea is to play d4. In addition, we want to get Black out of their theory!} 2.c3 2...Nc6 {Nc6, developing, is the most popular move. It is played 35% of times. It covers the d4 square somewhat, but we can still push with d4. And we will!} 3.d4 3...cxd4 {Black usually plays this trade. So let's just recapture. Now we have two strong central pawns! Not bad!} 4.cxd4 4...d5 {This is the most popular move, being played over 2 million times. Black wants to challenge our central pawns. We'll just take here, and gain some tempi on the queen.} 5.exd5 5...Qxd5 {Black can recapture and attack the d4-pawn. Black has two developed pieces, so they are quite active. Before we gain a tempo, we have to defend the d-pawn.} 6.Nf3 6...Bg4 {This pin is actually quite challenging. We'll break the pin and defend.} 7.Be2 7...Nf6 {Development is fine here.} 8.Nc3 {But we develop with that nice tempo on the queen.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 2: Black Plays 4...e6 or 4...d6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/a700329e-dc5f-4868-8f40-3cd568d80858/studies/68878e26-8453-4f1e-9168-dc1f26bada6c/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian Defense. And now we'll play c3. The main idea is to play d4. In addition, we want to get Black out of their theory!} 2.c3 2...Nc6 {Nc6, developing, is the most popular move. It does control the d4-square, but we can still push the pawn. And so we will!} 3.d4 3...cxd4 {Black usually trades here. We'll recapture and maintain two strong central pawns.} 4.cxd4 4...e6 {A move we should take seriously is 4...e6, played 27% of the time. Objectively, it's not bad. Pushing 5. d5 doesn't give White a big advantage because Black can capture (5...exd5). So ...e6 does prove to be useful. Therefore we'll simply develop.} 5.Nf3 5...d5 {The most natural continuation after ...e6 is ...d5. But we have a great game after simply pushing our e-pawn. This is a good advanced French Defense, where we are in complete control of the center and the f6-square. Black has to develop both kingside pieces, which isn't so easy.} 6.e5 6...Bb4+ {For example, many players go for 6...Bb4+, looking for quick development. But we can just block with the knight. Black never wants to capture on c3. They would have too many dark-squared weaknesses and White's structure wouldn't be bad.} 7.Nc3 7...Nge7 {Black's idea was to get the bishop out and then the knight out through e7. Their problem now is that they cannot castle because of a common tactic. 8. Bd3 sets up the Greek Gift.} 8.Bd3 8...O-O {And this is a common blunder, walking into our idea. Now Bxh7+ works!} 9.Bxh7+ 9...Kxh7 {Black may as well take. And after the knight check, all legal moves lose.} 10.Ng5+ 10...Kg8 {If the king goes backwards...} 11.Qh5 {White gets a powerfully winning attack following the themes of the Greek Gift Sacrifice.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 2: Black Plays 4...e6 or 4...d6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/a700329e-dc5f-4868-8f40-3cd568d80858/studies/68878e26-8453-4f1e-9168-dc1f26bada6c/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian Defense. And now we'll play c3. The main idea is to play d4. In addition, we want to get Black out of their theory!} 2.c3 2...Nc6 {Nc6, developing, is the most popular move. It does control the d4-square, but we can still push the pawn. And so we will!} 3.d4 3...cxd4 {Black usually trades here. We'll recapture and maintain two strong central pawns.} 4.cxd4 4...d6 {As a testament to people's lack of preparation, 4...d6 is a mistake already and it has been played more than 450,000 times at the amateur level. After d5 we are already better.} 5.d5 5...Ne5 {The most popular move is the worse one in this case. The knight is an easy target and we can get an advantage after chasing the knight with f4, gaining space.} 6.f4 6...Ng6 {The knight retreats. We should develop following normal opening principles. It's difficult for Black to untangle with this much less space.} 7.Nf3 7...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. Developing poses Black many problems: basically, e5 is a big threat now and it is hard to deal with.} 8.Nc3 8...e6 {Black may now challenge the center. Taking is great for our structure. ...Bxe6 is a blunder, so fxe6 is necessary.} 9.dxe6 9...fxe6 {And after this trade, there's no need to go for anything forcing just yet. We don't need to rush. Development is the most important thing. We'll develop our bishop.} 10.Bd3 10...Be7 {Black may think they'll be fine if they castle and develop. But they have a problem with the kingside knights! Here comes the h-pawn! White's moves are just easy and natural to find, while Black must play accurately to stay alive.} 11.h4 11...h5 {The natural ...h5 to prevent h5 from White allows a discovered attack.} 12.e5 {And this should be the end. White will win material.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 2: Black Plays 4...e6 or 4...d6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/a700329e-dc5f-4868-8f40-3cd568d80858/studies/68878e26-8453-4f1e-9168-dc1f26bada6c/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian Defense. And now we'll play c3. The main idea is to play d4. In addition, we want to get Black out of their theory!} 2.c3 2...Nc6 {Nc6, developing, is the most popular move. It does control the d4-square, but we can still push the pawn. And so we will!} 3.d4 3...cxd4 {Black usually trades here. We'll recapture and maintain two strong central pawns.} 4.cxd4 4...e6 {A move we should take seriously is 4...e6, played 27% of the time. Objectively, it's not bad. Pushing 5. d5 doesn't give White a big advantage because Black can capture (5...exd5). So ...e6 does prove to be useful. Therefore we'll simply develop.} 5.Nf3 5...d5 {The most natural continuation after ...e6 is ...d5. But we have a great game after simply pushing our e-pawn. This is a good advanced French Defense, where we are in complete control of the center and the f6-square. Black has to develop both kingside pieces, which isn't so easy.} 6.e5 6...Nge7 {This is the correct approach. The bishop achieves nothing on b4. Once again, we should just get the knight out and develop.} 7.Nc3 7...Nf5 {Black's entire plan is to attack d4. To defend it, I suggest we go for an a3 configuration because it will help us play Na4 if Qb6.} 8.a3 8...Bd7 {d7 is the only square to develop the bishop to. As White, you cannot play Bd3 yet because of the pressure on d4. Therefore I like 9.h4. The threat is to remove the knight from its outpost.} 9.h4 9...h5 {Black should stop this. But if they do, that's perfectly fine.} 10.b4 {I like the option of expanding on the queenside. We control many more squares and create the b2-square for our bishop. If ...Qb6, Na4. We has a slight edge because of the extra space.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 2: Black Plays 4...e6 or 4...d6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/a700329e-dc5f-4868-8f40-3cd568d80858/studies/68878e26-8453-4f1e-9168-dc1f26bada6c/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian Defense. And now we'll play c3. The main idea is to play d4. In addition, we want to get Black out of their theory!} 2.c3 2...Nc6 {Nc6, developing, is the most popular move. It does control the d4-square, but we can still push the pawn. And so we will!} 3.d4 3...cxd4 {Black usually trades here. We'll recapture and maintain two strong central pawns.} 4.cxd4 4...d6 {As a testament to people's lack of preparation, 4...d6 is a mistake already and it has been played more than 450,000 times at the amateur level. After d5 we are already better.} 5.d5 5...Nb8 {This move is less popular but it causes less harm in Black's position. There's no f4 with a tempo, so we should just develop naturally with Nc3.} 6.Nc3 6...Nf6 {Since ...d6 protects the e5-square, people may go for ...Nf6. We should continue with our development with Nf3.} 7.Nf3 7...g6 {Because the pawns impede the bishop's movement you may see ...g6 for Black to develop it. I like to control the g4-square with h3. That way we make it harder for Black to develop.} 8.h3 8...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. c4 is a good square for our bishop.} 9.Bc4 9...O-O {Black castles.} 10.O-O {And so do we. I like White's space advantage. This is what the Alapin is meant to give you if your opponents don't respond in the best way. We can play moves like Re1, Be3 and a4 to gain space. This is advantageous according to the engine, but there isn't a concrete way forward. We'll focus on piece placement and maybe play for f4 later on.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Other 2nd Moves / Study 1: Black Plays 2...d6 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/7f96fd54-aa0e-4d2b-9938-be34997c5edb/studies/b6b5efcd-b2a4-4e73-9658-0e20f2bf4b0a/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian Defense. And now c3 is the Alapin. The main idea is to play d4. In addition, we want to get Black out of their theory!} 2.c3 2...d6 {2...d6 is the second most popular move. It is played 20% of times at the amateur level in over 2.5 million games in the database. Let's play d4 when we are allowed!} 3.d4 3...cxd4 {We must always contemplate this capture. We'll of course recapture and get our two central pawns.} 4.cxd4 4...Nf6 {Black develops and attacks e4. We'll develop and defend the pawn.} 5.Nc3 5...g6 {People usually continue with g6. Against this, I suggest we just respond with normal development, achieving a classical central formation with our knights and pawns.} 6.Nf3 6...Bg7 {Black usually fianchettoes. We'll develop with check and a tempo.} 7.Bb5+ 7...Nc6 {This is a more basic type of blunder. It's a self-pin on a vulnerable square. d5 begs to be played!} 8.d5 8...a6 {Black may try to attack the bishop and force it off the diagonal. But we'll just retreat the bishop and induce Black to weaken themselves.} 9.Ba4 9...b5 {Black's point is that they break the pin, but in the process they lose a very important pawn. We'll simply capture the knight.} 10.dxc6 10...bxa4 {Black takes on a4.} 11.Qxa4 {And this should be a comfortable win. You're going to want to go for Bg5, 0-0 and Rac1 to support the c-pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Other 2nd Moves / Study 1: Black Plays 2...d6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/7f96fd54-aa0e-4d2b-9938-be34997c5edb/studies/b6b5efcd-b2a4-4e73-9658-0e20f2bf4b0a/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian Defense. And now c3 is the Alapin. The main idea is to play d4. In addition, we want to get Black out of their theory!} 2.c3 2...d6 {2...d6 is the second most popular move. It is played 20% of times at the amateur level in over 2.5 million games in the database. Let's play d4 when we are allowed!} 3.d4 3...cxd4 {We must always contemplate this capture. We'll of course recapture and get our two central pawns.} 4.cxd4 4...Nf6 {Black develops and attacks e4. We'll develop and defend the pawn.} 5.Nc3 5...g6 {People usually continue with g6. Against this, I suggest we just respond with normal development, achieving a classical central formation with our knights and pawns.} 6.Nf3 6...Bg7 {Black usually fianchettoes. We'll develop with check and a tempo.} 7.Bb5+ 7...Nbd7 {This move is problematic.} 8.e5 {At this point, 8. e5 is practically winning. Black's knights are in a tangle. There's even a strong plan of e6 to weaken the pawns!} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Other 2nd Moves / Study 1: Black Plays 2...d6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/7f96fd54-aa0e-4d2b-9938-be34997c5edb/studies/b6b5efcd-b2a4-4e73-9658-0e20f2bf4b0a/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian Defense. And now c3 is the Alapin. The main idea is to play d4. In addition, we want to get Black out of their theory!} 2.c3 2...d6 {2...d6 is the second most popular move. It is played 20% of times at the amateur level in over 2.5 million games in the database. Let's play d4 when we are allowed!} 3.d4 3...cxd4 {We must always contemplate this capture. We'll of course recapture and get our two central pawns.} 4.cxd4 4...Nf6 {Black develops and attacks e4. We'll develop and defend the pawn.} 5.Nc3 5...g6 {People usually continue with g6. Against this, I suggest we just respond with normal development, achieving a classical central formation with our knights and pawns.} 6.Nf3 6...Bg7 {Black usually fianchettoes. We'll develop with check and a tempo.} 7.Bb5+ 7...Bd7 {If the bishop blocks... We can start using our mobile center!} 8.e5 8...dxe5 {Black may trade, but we have another pawn that can take its place. We'll recapture and attack the knight. Black's position is crumbling.} 9.dxe5 9...Ng4 {The knight and bishop attack e5. So we'll develop and defend the pawn.} 10.Bf4 10...Nc6 {10...Nc6 looks normal, too. But our main idea stands true: we will play Qe2, holding on to e5 and getting ready for h3.} 11.Qe2 11...O-O {If Black castles now, we can make use of the open d-file.} 12.Rd1 {It's not easy to deal with this. White is completely winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Other 2nd Moves / Study 1: Black Plays 2...d6 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/7f96fd54-aa0e-4d2b-9938-be34997c5edb/studies/b6b5efcd-b2a4-4e73-9658-0e20f2bf4b0a/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian Defense. And now c3 is the Alapin. The main idea is to play d4. In addition, we want to get Black out of their theory!} 2.c3 2...d6 {2...d6 is the second most popular move. It is played 20% of times at the amateur level in over 2.5 million games in the database. Let's play d4 when we are allowed!} 3.d4 3...cxd4 {We must always contemplate this capture. We'll of course recapture and get our two central pawns.} 4.cxd4 4...Nf6 {Black develops and attacks e4. We'll develop and defend the pawn.} 5.Nc3 5...g6 {People usually continue with g6. Against this, I suggest we just respond with normal development, achieving a classical central formation with our knights and pawns.} 6.Nf3 6...Bg4 {You may also see this pin. But it isn't effective at all. Black is offering the bishop pair, in essence.} 7.h3 7...Bxf3 {Taking is the most logical. We should of course recapture.} 8.Qxf3 8...Nc6 {If they develop and attack our pawn, d5 is strong. This is the nice thing about having a strong and mobile pawn duo in the center.} 9.d5 9...Ne5 {Even though Black can centralize and attack our queen, it's just a tempo. It doesn't change the long-term aspects of the position. One of these aspects is that we have more space and central control. It is good to insert this check. Black will have to ponder what knight they'll block with.} 10.Bb5+ 10...Nfd7 {If they block with the f-knight, we have a strong idea against the e5-knight.} 11.Qe3 {f4 is coming. Black has to find a way to untangle or they'll lose a piece! If a6, your bishop goes to e2 and you're better because of the bishop pair and extra space.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Other 2nd Moves / Study 1: Black Plays 2...d6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/7f96fd54-aa0e-4d2b-9938-be34997c5edb/studies/b6b5efcd-b2a4-4e73-9658-0e20f2bf4b0a/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian Defense. And now c3 is the Alapin. The main idea is to play d4. In addition, we want to get Black out of their theory!} 2.c3 2...d6 {2...d6 is the second most popular move. It is played 20% of times at the amateur level in over 2.5 million games in the database. Let's play d4 when we are allowed!} 3.d4 3...cxd4 {We must always contemplate this capture. We'll of course recapture and get our two central pawns.} 4.cxd4 4...Nf6 {Black develops and attacks e4. We'll develop and defend the pawn.} 5.Nc3 5...g6 {People usually continue with g6. Against this, I suggest we just respond with normal development, achieving a classical central formation with our knights and pawns.} 6.Nf3 6...Bg7 {Black usually fianchettoes. We'll develop with check and a tempo.} 7.Bb5+ 7...Bd7 {If the bishop blocks... We can start using our mobile center!} 8.e5 8...dxe5 {Black may trade, but we have another pawn that can take its place. We'll recapture and attack the knight. Black's position is crumbling.} 9.dxe5 9...Ng4 {The knight and bishop attack e5. So we'll develop and defend the pawn.} 10.Bf4 10...O-O {This move is natural, and so is Nc6. But against this all we have to do is attack the knight. h3 pushes it to the edge of the board.} 11.h3 11...Bxb5 {Black could always insert this capture. Nevertheless, exchanging queens leaves us with a winning advantage...} 12.Qxd8 12...Rxd8 {Black recaptures.} 13.Nxb5 {And we threaten Nc7 as well as hxg4. White is winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Other 2nd Moves / Study 1: Black Plays 2...d6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/7f96fd54-aa0e-4d2b-9938-be34997c5edb/studies/b6b5efcd-b2a4-4e73-9658-0e20f2bf4b0a/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian Defense. And now c3 is the Alapin. The main idea is to play d4. In addition, we want to get Black out of their theory!} 2.c3 2...d6 {2...d6 is the second most popular move. It is played 20% of times at the amateur level in over 2.5 million games in the database. Let's play d4 when we are allowed!} 3.d4 3...cxd4 {We must always contemplate this capture. We'll of course recapture and get our two central pawns.} 4.cxd4 4...Nf6 {Black develops and attacks e4. We'll develop and defend the pawn.} 5.Nc3 5...g6 {People usually continue with g6. Against this, I suggest we just respond with normal development, achieving a classical central formation with our knights and pawns.} 6.Nf3 6...Bg7 {Black usually fianchettoes. We'll develop with check and a tempo.} 7.Bb5+ 7...Bd7 {If the bishop blocks... We can start using our mobile center!} 8.e5 8...dxe5 {Black may trade, but we have another pawn that can take its place. We'll recapture and attack the knight. Black's position is crumbling.} 9.dxe5 9...Ng4 {The knight and bishop attack e5. So we'll develop and defend the pawn.} 10.Bf4 10...O-O {This move is natural, and so is Nc6. But against this all we have to do is attack the knight. h3 pushes it to the edge of the board.} 11.h3 11...Nh6 {On h6 it still has the f5-square, but we don't need to have tunnel vision with g4 stuff at this moment. We should trade on d7 first.} 12.Bxd7 12...Qxd7 {After this recapture, what you'll often see is a queen trade. First we'll castle, though. There's no need to initiate the trade.} 13.O-O 13...Qxd1 {If Black does trade queens, the game simplifies in our favor.} 14.Raxd1 {It's hard for Black to make any progress. In fact, if they continue with Nc6, we've got Rd7. Rd7 is hard to stop and we'll attack everything on their 7th rank. This is winning, too.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Other 2nd Moves / Study 1: Black Plays 2...d6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/7f96fd54-aa0e-4d2b-9938-be34997c5edb/studies/b6b5efcd-b2a4-4e73-9658-0e20f2bf4b0a/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian Defense. And now c3 is the Alapin. The main idea is to play d4. In addition, we want to get Black out of their theory!} 2.c3 2...d6 {2...d6 is the second most popular move. It is played 20% of times at the amateur level in over 2.5 million games in the database. Let's play d4 when we are allowed!} 3.d4 3...cxd4 {We must always contemplate this capture. We'll of course recapture and get our two central pawns.} 4.cxd4 4...Nf6 {Black develops and attacks e4. We'll develop and defend the pawn.} 5.Nc3 5...g6 {People usually continue with g6. Against this, I suggest we just respond with normal development, achieving a classical central formation with our knights and pawns.} 6.Nf3 6...Bg7 {Black usually fianchettoes. We'll develop with check and a tempo.} 7.Bb5+ 7...Bd7 {If the bishop blocks... We can start using our mobile center!} 8.e5 8...Bxb5 {This move is a blunder.} 9.exf6 {We have a strong in-between move, winning a piece.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Other 2nd Moves / Study 1: Black Plays 2...d6 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/7f96fd54-aa0e-4d2b-9938-be34997c5edb/studies/b6b5efcd-b2a4-4e73-9658-0e20f2bf4b0a/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian Defense. And now c3 is the Alapin. The main idea is to play d4. In addition, we want to get Black out of their theory!} 2.c3 2...d6 {2...d6 is the second most popular move. It is played 20% of times at the amateur level in over 2.5 million games in the database. Let's play d4 when we are allowed!} 3.d4 3...Nf6 {I suppose we could explore what to play against this knight move. It's been played more than 25,000 times on the database. I like taking on c5. It's the most practical try and Black is prone to blunder against it.} 4.dxc5 4...dxc5 {Taking on c5 isn't good at all. We can trade queens and make Black lose castling rights.} 5.Qxd8+ 5...Kxd8 {With the king misplaced, this should be an easy to play middlegame.} 6.Bd3 {We can just go ahead and defend our pawn. We're a lot better after simple moves like Nf3 and 0-0.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Other 2nd Moves / Study 1: Black Plays 2...d6 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/7f96fd54-aa0e-4d2b-9938-be34997c5edb/studies/b6b5efcd-b2a4-4e73-9658-0e20f2bf4b0a/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian Defense. And now c3 is the Alapin. The main idea is to play d4. In addition, we want to get Black out of their theory!} 2.c3 2...d6 {2...d6 is the second most popular move. It is played 20% of times at the amateur level in over 2.5 million games in the database. Let's play d4 when we are allowed!} 3.d4 3...Nf6 {I suppose we could explore what to play against this knight move. It's been played more than 25,000 times on the database. I like taking on c5. It's the most practical try and Black is prone to blunder against it.} 4.dxc5 4...Nxe4 {For example, they may think this is a good capture.} 5.Qa4+ {But this loses a knight for free.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Other 2nd Moves / Study 2: Black Plays 2...e6 (Part 1) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/7f96fd54-aa0e-4d2b-9938-be34997c5edb/studies/f69e69a7-2ee9-4ff8-81e3-b415d0c6e4d0/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian Defense. And now c3 is the Alapin. The main idea is to play d4. In addition, we want to get Black out of their theory!} 2.c3 2...e6 {e6 can be an idea from Black to play d5. 2...e6 has been played 14% of times in around 1,700,000 games. We'll play d4.} 3.d4 3...d5 {Black could go for the immediate d5. I like to recommend capturing on d5 because the alternative of e5 would transpose into an Advanced French! Let's play something unique!} 4.exd5 4...exd5 {Most people recapture with the e-pawn. I like to insert Bb5+ to speed up our development.} 5.Bb5+ 5...Nc6 {Let's start with Nc6. There's nothing wrong for Black at this point. We should develop with Nf3 and support our center.} 6.Nf3 6...Nf6 {It is very likely that Black will continue in the same way. We'll castle and get ready to play Re1 at some point.} 7.O-O 7...Be7 {Black is advised to get their king to safety quickly. The detail here is that we can make Black lose a tempo. If we take on c5, Black has to move their bishop again!} 8.dxc5 8...Bxc5 {Black recaptures on c5. With Bg5, we can even get a pin at the end of this sequence, inviting the bishop to move yet again to e7.} 9.Bg5 9...O-O {If they don't defend the center, we can remove the defender. We'll take on f6.} 10.Bxf6 10...Qxf6 {And because we played 2. c3 our b-pawn is safe from the black queen.} 11.Qxd5 {We can emerge up a pawn and with better development. Black will have to spend a move to save their bishop. Meanwhile, we can bring our queen's knight to d2 and even e4, plus our rooks will be arriving shortly on the central d- and e-files.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Other 2nd Moves / Study 2: Black Plays 2...e6 (Part 1) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/7f96fd54-aa0e-4d2b-9938-be34997c5edb/studies/f69e69a7-2ee9-4ff8-81e3-b415d0c6e4d0/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian Defense. And now c3 is the Alapin. The main idea is to play d4. In addition, we want to get Black out of their theory!} 2.c3 2...e6 {e6 can be an idea from Black to play d5. 2...e6 has been played 14% of times in around 1,700,000 games. We'll play d4.} 3.d4 3...d5 {Black could go for the immediate d5. I like to recommend capturing on d5 because the alternative of e5 would transpose into an Advanced French! Let's play something unique!} 4.exd5 4...exd5 {Most people recapture with the e-pawn. I like to insert Bb5+ to speed up our development.} 5.Bb5+ 5...Nc6 {Let's start with Nc6. There's nothing wrong for Black at this point. We should develop with Nf3 and support our center.} 6.Nf3 6...Nf6 {It is very likely that Black will continue in the same way. We'll castle and get ready to play Re1 at some point.} 7.O-O 7...Be7 {Black is advised to get their king to safety quickly. The detail here is that we can make Black lose a tempo. If we take on c5, Black has to move their bishop again!} 8.dxc5 8...Bxc5 {Black recaptures on c5. With Bg5, we can even get a pin at the end of this sequence, inviting the bishop to move yet again to e7.} 9.Bg5 9...Be6 {Black can also defend d5. If this happens, we should just complete development. We'll bring our queen to d3, leaving the d2 square for our other knight.} 10.Qd3 10...O-O {Black castles, and now I like to simply develop.} 11.Nbd2 {We're playing for control of the d4-square. We have to keep in mind everything we know about restricting the IQP and playing against it. Because our development is solid, we'll be prepared to deal with any aggressive plans Black may have.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Other 2nd Moves / Study 2: Black Plays 2...e6 (Part 1) / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/7f96fd54-aa0e-4d2b-9938-be34997c5edb/studies/f69e69a7-2ee9-4ff8-81e3-b415d0c6e4d0/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian Defense. And now c3 is the Alapin. The main idea is to play d4. In addition, we want to get Black out of their theory!} 2.c3 2...e6 {e6 can be an idea from Black to play d5. 2...e6 has been played 14% of times in around 1,700,000 games. We'll play d4.} 3.d4 3...d5 {Black could go for the immediate d5. I like to recommend capturing on d5 because the alternative of e5 would transpose into an Advanced French! Let's play something unique!} 4.exd5 4...exd5 {Most people recapture with the e-pawn. I like to insert Bb5+ to speed up our development.} 5.Bb5+ 5...Bd7 {This is the other way of blocking the check. It's a lot more confrontational. We should just trade and prioritize our development.} 6.Bxd7+ 6...Nxd7 {You could also see this recapture. We'll simply develop.} 7.Nf3 7...Ngf6 {Black does the same. We're not taking on c5 yet because we don't want to help Black develop with Bxc5. Hence we'll just castle.} 8.O-O 8...Be7 {But as soon as this bishop moves, you know what to do! We make the capture on c5!} 9.dxc5 9...Nxc5 {In this variation, Bxc5 is unplayable because of Re1+. After Nxc5, we can go for a pin on the bishop on e7, and Re1 is still our choice.} 10.Re1 10...O-O {However, Black can quickly castle.} 11.Na3 {This is very important. We want absolute control of the d4-square because we're playing against the IQP. Nc2 to d4 is an idea. Be3 is a move that also fits this strategy. This position is about equal, but I'm sure you can outplay Black here if you play positionally.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Other 2nd Moves / Study 2: Black Plays 2...e6 (Part 1) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/7f96fd54-aa0e-4d2b-9938-be34997c5edb/studies/f69e69a7-2ee9-4ff8-81e3-b415d0c6e4d0/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian Defense. And now c3 is the Alapin. The main idea is to play d4. In addition, we want to get Black out of their theory!} 2.c3 2...e6 {e6 can be an idea from Black to play d5. 2...e6 has been played 14% of times in around 1,700,000 games. We'll play d4.} 3.d4 3...cxd4 {This trade is popular. We'll just recapture, of course.} 4.cxd4 4...d5 {And d5 can be played now. Let's just gain space with e5.} 5.e5 5...f6 {Some players are very eager to challenge the center. In reality, normal moves like Nge7-f5 are better, but they take longer. Nc6 would be similar to lines we have examine earlier. After ...f6, we can get a dominant position quickly. Black isn't really undermining e5 because our knight can jump into e5.} 6.Nf3 6...fxe5 {Black trades. We recapture and centralize our knight in a dominating outpost.} 7.Nxe5 7...Nf6 {Black prevents Qh5+. Now the top engine line is for us give a bishop check. I like this because if Nbd7 Black's coordination is really bad and they are stuck in a pin.} 8.Bb5+ 8...Bd7 {If they block with the bishop, we can enjoy a better middlegame by trading. This gives us the bishop pair. We also remove the light-squared bishop that was defending the e6-weakness.} 9.Nxd7 9...Nbxd7 {Black recaptures and develops.} 10.O-O {But we get our king into safety just in time. I really like this position for White. We have the bishop pair for the endgame, and we'll soon be applying pressure to the weak pawn on e6.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Other 2nd Moves / Study 2: Black Plays 2...e6 (Part 1) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/7f96fd54-aa0e-4d2b-9938-be34997c5edb/studies/f69e69a7-2ee9-4ff8-81e3-b415d0c6e4d0/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian Defense. And now c3 is the Alapin. The main idea is to play d4. In addition, we want to get Black out of their theory!} 2.c3 2...e6 {e6 can be an idea from Black to play d5. 2...e6 has been played 14% of times in around 1,700,000 games. We'll play d4.} 3.d4 3...d5 {Black could go for the immediate d5. I like to recommend capturing on d5 because the alternative of e5 would transpose into an Advanced French! Let's play something unique!} 4.exd5 4...exd5 {Most people recapture with the e-pawn. I like to insert Bb5+ to speed up our development.} 5.Bb5+ 5...Bd7 {This is the other way of blocking the check. It's a lot more confrontational. We should just trade and prioritize our development.} 6.Bxd7+ 6...Qxd7 {This recapture makes a lot of sense. Black delays knight development in order to bring it to c6. We should develop our g1-knight.} 7.Nf3 7...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. This is rather equal.} 8.O-O {We're going to be centralizing our knight and developing our bishop next. The rook belongs on the open e-file. We'll want to keep our pieces in touch with the d4-pawn because we would prefer to not end up with an IQP just like Black. Those positions are too equal with both sides having the same weakness.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Other 2nd Moves / Study 2: Black Plays 2...e6 (Part 1) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/7f96fd54-aa0e-4d2b-9938-be34997c5edb/studies/f69e69a7-2ee9-4ff8-81e3-b415d0c6e4d0/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian Defense. And now c3 is the Alapin. The main idea is to play d4. In addition, we want to get Black out of their theory!} 2.c3 2...e6 {e6 can be an idea from Black to play d5. 2...e6 has been played 14% of times in around 1,700,000 games. We'll play d4.} 3.d4 3...cxd4 {This trade is popular. We'll just recapture, of course.} 4.cxd4 4...d5 {And d5 can be played now. Let's just gain space with e5.} 5.e5 5...Bb4+ {Development with Bb4+ is almost never good in these advanced French structures. Nevertheless, many go for it. We'll just block on c3 with the knight.} 6.Nc3 6...Ne7 {Black's entire plan is to get castled. We'll begin to set up the Greek Gift.} 7.Bd3 7...O-O {Black can castle here. It's perfectly fine, as we don't have a knight on f3 yet.} 8.Nf3 {But now we truly threaten Bxh7+. Black probably has to move a pawn like 8...f6 or 8...h6 to avoid the gift. After which there are some good plans with a3 and 0-0. We don't really mind ...Bxc3+ when Black gives up the bishop pair.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Other 2nd Moves / Study 3: Black Plays 2...e6 (Part 2) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/7f96fd54-aa0e-4d2b-9938-be34997c5edb/studies/82510520-ad68-4964-b4fb-4af04ac82f05/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian Defense. And now c3 is the Alapin. We'd like to expand with d4.} 2.c3 2...e6 {Black wants to play ...d5. We'll go d4.} 3.d4 3...d5 {Black plays their planned d5. I like to recommend capturing on d5 because the alternative of e5 transposes into an Advanced French.} 4.exd5 4...Qxd5 {Fewer people take with the queen but it is a valid move. And here I like Na3. There are some neat ideas that include Nb5.} 5.Na3 5...a6 {Black could also attempt to stop Nb5 altogether. Since our fork idea is stopped, we should develop with Nf3.} 6.Nf3 6...Nf6 {Black could develop their knight to f6. But Nc4 is good here as well.} 7.Nc4 7...Nbd7 {Black has to protect the b6-square. Now a4 is a very instructive move. It keeps the knight stable (no pun intended) on c4.} 8.a4 8...Be7 {Black can prepare castling. And 9. a5 really preserves the knight's stable on c4.} 9.a5 9...O-O {Black castles. But now we can get ready to castle, too, with Be2. Black has a serious lack of space and an awkward setup. The queen is in front of the whole army and the c8-bishop is buried.} 10.Be2 10...cxd4 {If they take in the center, we can propose a queen trade.} 11.Qxd4 {Black is cramped, and White stands better. 0-0 and Be3 are coming up next. We'll want to play Rfd1 to control the d-file at some point. We want to leave the rook on a1 because it helps defend a5.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Other 2nd Moves / Study 3: Black Plays 2...e6 (Part 2) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/7f96fd54-aa0e-4d2b-9938-be34997c5edb/studies/82510520-ad68-4964-b4fb-4af04ac82f05/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian Defense. And now c3 is the Alapin. We'd like to expand with d4.} 2.c3 2...e6 {Black wants to play ...d5. We'll go d4.} 3.d4 3...d5 {Black plays their planned d5. I like to recommend capturing on d5 because the alternative of e5 transposes into an Advanced French.} 4.exd5 4...Qxd5 {Fewer people take with the queen but it is a valid move. And here I like Na3. There are some neat ideas that include Nb5.} 5.Na3 5...cxd4 {Black may take in the center. We'll play Nb5, and threaten a fork on c7.} 6.Nb5 6...Na6 {Black can cover the c7-square. But we can capture on d4.} 7.Qxd4 7...Qxd4 {This trade is reasonable. We'll take back with the knight. Black is fairly equal here, but the knight on a6 is awkward.} 8.Nxd4 8...Nf6 {Black develops. We'll develop as well.} 9.Ngf3 9...Be7 {Black gets ready to castle. But here we can give a disruptive check on b5.} 10.Bb5+ 10...Bd7 {After the bishop goes to d7, we can proceed and take on a6. We intend to make a target out of the d7-bishop.} 11.Bxa6 11...bxa6 {Black recaptures. And here's a tempo on the bishop.} 12.Ne5 12...Bc8 {The bishop retreats, but our knights coordinate beautifully. Our next move prevents Black from castling.} 13.Ndc6 13...Bd6 {Our knights have caused some disturbances. The bishop has to move again. I like Bg5 for quick development. If ...0-0, we can take on f6 and damage the pawns. We would probably play a combination of Nc4 and Bxf6.} 14.Bg5 14...h6 {I really like that if a natural move like 14...h6, we can start making use of our lead in development. We'll castle, attacking the d6-bishop. If Bxe5, there's Rd8#.} 15.O-O-O 15...Bc7 {Supposing the bishop retreats...} 16.Be3 {We can do the same. This is just a worse endgame for Black. Our knights are annoying on those squares. If 16...0-0, Ne7+ and Nxc8 is always good. There are even variations with an invasion of the 7th rank after both the c8-bishop and f6 knight come off the board.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Other 2nd Moves / Study 3: Black Plays 2...e6 (Part 2) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/7f96fd54-aa0e-4d2b-9938-be34997c5edb/studies/82510520-ad68-4964-b4fb-4af04ac82f05/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian Defense. And now c3 is the Alapin. We'd like to expand with d4.} 2.c3 2...e6 {Black wants to play ...d5. We'll go d4.} 3.d4 3...d5 {Black plays their planned d5. I like to recommend capturing on d5 because the alternative of e5 transposes into an Advanced French.} 4.exd5 4...Qxd5 {Fewer people take with the queen but it is a valid move. And here I like Na3. There are some neat ideas that include Nb5.} 5.Na3 5...Nf6 {Many players are not concerned with the central tension or the Nb5 move. They just develop. We'll play Nb5 and threaten a fork.} 6.Nb5 6...Na6 {Na6 is a good way for Black to develop and defend the c7-square.} 7.Nf3 {We should just continue by developing our minor pieces. If cxd4, Nbxd4 is a good move.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Other 2nd Moves / Study 3: Black Plays 2...e6 (Part 2) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/7f96fd54-aa0e-4d2b-9938-be34997c5edb/studies/82510520-ad68-4964-b4fb-4af04ac82f05/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian Defense. And now c3 is the Alapin. We'd like to expand with d4.} 2.c3 2...e6 {Black wants to play ...d5. We'll go d4.} 3.d4 3...d5 {Black plays their planned d5. I like to recommend capturing on d5 because the alternative of e5 transposes into an Advanced French.} 4.exd5 4...Qxd5 {Fewer people take with the queen but it is a valid move. And here I like Na3. There are some neat ideas that include Nb5.} 5.Na3 5...a6 {Black could also attempt to stop Nb5 altogether. Since our fork idea is stopped, we should develop with Nf3.} 6.Nf3 6...cxd4 {After ...cxd4, there's a nice knight move to complicate matters a bit. We'll play Nc4, with the main idea being Nb6.} 7.Nc4 7...Nd7 {Black is advised to develop and cover the b6-square. Capturing on c3 ran into 8. Qxd5, when Nb6 forks the rook and bishop.} 8.Nxd4 {But we can use our f-knight to capture on d4. We should play the simple Be2, intending 0-0. Black could choose to take on g2, but after Bf3 all of our pieces are active. It's a risky pawn for Black to accept!} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Other 2nd Moves / Study 3: Black Plays 2...e6 (Part 2) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/7f96fd54-aa0e-4d2b-9938-be34997c5edb/studies/82510520-ad68-4964-b4fb-4af04ac82f05/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {The Sicilian Defense. And now c3 is the Alapin. We'd like to expand with d4.} 2.c3 2...e6 {Black wants to play ...d5. We'll go d4.} 3.d4 3...d5 {Black plays their planned d5. I like to recommend capturing on d5 because the alternative of e5 transposes into an Advanced French.} 4.exd5 4...Qxd5 {Fewer people take with the queen but it is a valid move. And here I like Na3. There are some neat ideas that include Nb5.} 5.Na3 5...cxd4 {Black may take in the center. We'll play Nb5, and threaten a fork on c7.} 6.Nb5 6...Qd8 {Black has the option of going all the way back to cover c7. But we'll develop and add an attacker.} 7.Bf4 7...Na6 {Black has to defend c7. With Qa4, we threaten a discovered check.} 8.Qa4 8...Bd7 {Black pins our knight.} 9.Qxd4 {But Qxd4 and White is better. Our plan should be something like Nf3 and 0-0-0 to place your rook on the open d-file.} *

