[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Other 2nd Moves / Study 4: Black Plays 2...g6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/7f96fd54-aa0e-4d2b-9938-be34997c5edb/studies/ab5fbe6a-ecf0-4196-9321-55b3159319f4/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 2.c3 2...g6 3.d4 3...cxd4 4.cxd4 4...Bg7 5.Nc3 5...d6 6.Nf3 6...Bg4 7.Bb5+ 7...Nd7 8.Be2 *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Lines (2...d5 and 2...Nf6) / Study 1: Black Plays 2...d5, with 4...cxd4 or 4...Nc6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/f9769969-d465-42af-80b0-4a8c5ed12f82/studies/aee17933-6c8a-499d-b40f-e8d8c7750c55/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll play c3 and go for the Alapin.} 2.c3 2...d5 {Black breaks in the center with 2...d5. They don't want to suffer with a lack of space, so they expand in the center. Here we should capture.} 3.exd5 3...Qxd5 {And after the queen recaptures... We can play our d4 idea.} 4.d4 4...Nc6 {Black could develop the knight to c6 at this point. Preparing the c4 and d5 expansion is good. We must first support d4, so we'll play Nf3.} 5.Nf3 5...Bg4 {Black can pin our knight. But it's an empty threat since after Be2 they're never going to be taking on f3.} 6.Be2 6...O-O-O {Black castles. Black won't capture on f3, hence h3 is a free move.} 7.h3 7...Bh5 {The bishop goes backwards to h5. We'll push our pawns forward, starting with c4.} 8.c4 8...Qd7 {The queen moves back.} 9.d5 {And we attack the knight, benefitting from a closed d-file for the moment. We're going to be completing our development: 0-0, Nc3 and bishop to f4 or g5. White is better.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Lines (2...d5 and 2...Nf6) / Study 1: Black Plays 2...d5, with 4...cxd4 or 4...Nc6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/f9769969-d465-42af-80b0-4a8c5ed12f82/studies/aee17933-6c8a-499d-b40f-e8d8c7750c55/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll play c3 and go for the Alapin.} 2.c3 2...d5 {Black breaks in the center with 2...d5. They don't want to suffer with a lack of space, so they expand in the center. Here we should capture.} 3.exd5 3...Qxd5 {And after the queen recaptures... We can play our d4 idea.} 4.d4 4...cxd4 {Black trades. We'll recapture. After our recapture, 5...Nc6 would transpose back to chapter 1.} 5.cxd4 5...e5 {Black undermines the center right away with ...e5. It's not all that bad to play this way. The best move is to support the pawn with Nf3.} 6.Nf3 6...e4 {Black could also advance the pawn and attack the knight. We'll develop and win a tempo on the queen.} 7.Nc3 7...Bb4 {Black pins our attacker. Now we'll place our knight on an excellent location with Ne5. We prepare Bc4.} 8.Ne5 8...Nc6 {After knight c6, Black attacks d4. We should just trade on c6.} 9.Nxc6 9...bxc6 {Black recaptures.} 10.Be2 {And we're going to castle soon. a3 is a good move since Bxc3 is welcomed. Qa4 is also a nice idea to apply pressure and play Bc4 if possible.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Lines (2...d5 and 2...Nf6) / Study 1: Black Plays 2...d5, with 4...cxd4 or 4...Nc6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/f9769969-d465-42af-80b0-4a8c5ed12f82/studies/aee17933-6c8a-499d-b40f-e8d8c7750c55/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll play c3 and go for the Alapin.} 2.c3 2...d5 {Black breaks in the center with 2...d5. They don't want to suffer with a lack of space, so they expand in the center. Here we should capture.} 3.exd5 3...Nf6 {This move is also possible. Black seeks rapid development. Now Bb5+ is a good idea, to develop while disrupting Black's coordination.} 4.Bb5+ 4...Bd7 {The bishop blocks. This is usually what you'll see. The trade is good. We have gotten rid of the light-squared bishop, so now we can play c4.} 5.Bxd7+ 5...Qxd7 {Black recaptures. We'll defend our extra pawn with c4, now that a pawn on c4 no longer interferes with the scope of our light-squared bishop. With d5 defended, many players will try to attack it somehow.} 6.c4 6...e6 {Black may try to undermine the structure. They really want to get rid of the d-pawn to develop the knight to c6. But Qe2 causes their plan to backfire.} 7.Qe2 7...Be7 {The bishop breaks the pin. But now we can trade on e6 and create an isolated pawn on the e-file.} 8.dxe6 8...fxe6 {Black recaptures. We develop our king's knight. Even though we gave Black the c6-square, it isn't without a price.} 9.Nf3 9...Nc6 {Black develops. We castle. e6 is a permanent weakness and we're not worried about ...Nd4.} 10.O-O 10...O-O {And Black castles.} 11.d3 {11. d3 should be our go-to move: your structure is healthy and we are a pawn to the good. We can follow up with Nc3 and Be3. Sure, d3 is a backwards pawn, but it's possible Black won't be able to target it effectively without the light-squared bishop. Our structure is better: the e6-pawn is a long-term weakness.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Lines (2...d5 and 2...Nf6) / Study 1: Black Plays 2...d5, with 4...cxd4 or 4...Nc6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/f9769969-d465-42af-80b0-4a8c5ed12f82/studies/aee17933-6c8a-499d-b40f-e8d8c7750c55/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll play c3 and go for the Alapin.} 2.c3 2...d5 {Black breaks in the center with 2...d5. They don't want to suffer with a lack of space, so they expand in the center. Here we should capture.} 3.exd5 3...Qxd5 {And after the queen recaptures... We can play our d4 idea.} 4.d4 4...cxd4 {Black trades. We'll recapture. After our recapture, 5...Nc6 would transpose back to chapter 1.} 5.cxd4 5...e5 {Black undermines the center right away with ...e5. It's not all that bad to play this way. The best move is to support the pawn with Nf3.} 6.Nf3 6...exd4 {Black can capture in the center. This move looks logical, but the end result will be more development for White. After some trades, we will have a knight in the center.} 7.Qxd4 7...Qxd4 {Black takes. And we'll recapture with our knight on d4.} 8.Nxd4 8...Nf6 {Black develops their knight to f6. If 8...Nc6, 9. Nb5. Now Bb5+ is a good move for us. Black cannot block with Nc6.} 9.Bb5+ 9...Bd7 {Black blocks the check with the bishop. We'll castle. Our lead in development is evident. Re1 is coming.} 10.O-O 10...Bc5 {Black can gain a tempo on the knight, but we have a check. Re1+ is powerful. Black has to give up castling rights and they have to choose whether to play ...Kd8 or ...Kf8.} 11.Re1+ 11...Kf8 {This is the better of the two king moves because the d-file isn't safe. But the entire situation isn't optimal for Black's coordination. We'll now trade on d7, as our d4-knight (which supports our bishop) is attacked.} 12.Bxd7 12...Nbxd7 {And when Black recaptures, we can retreat the knight to safety.} 13.Nb3 {You will continue with Nc3 and h3. You can always defend f2 with Ne4 and h3 to kick the knight out of g4 if it goes there. You should have a small edge in this endgame.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Lines (2...d5 and 2...Nf6) / Study 1: Black Plays 2...d5, with 4...cxd4 or 4...Nc6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/f9769969-d465-42af-80b0-4a8c5ed12f82/studies/aee17933-6c8a-499d-b40f-e8d8c7750c55/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll play c3 and go for the Alapin.} 2.c3 2...d5 {Black breaks in the center with 2...d5. They don't want to suffer with a lack of space, so they expand in the center. Here we should capture.} 3.exd5 3...Qxd5 {And after the queen recaptures... We can play our d4 idea.} 4.d4 4...cxd4 {Black trades. We'll recapture. After our recapture, 5...Nc6 would transpose back to chapter 1.} 5.cxd4 5...Nf6 {Black can develop the knight to f6. We'll develop with Nc3 and gain a tempo on the queen.} 6.Nc3 6...Qd8 {The queen goes back to d8. Our d-pawn is giving us some extra space and central control.} 7.Nf3 {This is a very harmonious position. Although White doesn't have a huge advantage, we're more comfortable, have central control and better development. Expect to go Bc4 and 0-0 to start playing with the isolated pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Lines (2...d5 and 2...Nf6) / Study 2: Black Plays 2...d5 and 4...Nf6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/f9769969-d465-42af-80b0-4a8c5ed12f82/studies/08d50e70-dc88-4bd1-bbab-1e3276763aed/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. And we'll go for the Alapin.} 2.c3 2...d5 {Black breaks in the center with 2...d5. They don't want to suffer with a lack of space, so they expand in the center. Here we should capture.} 3.exd5 3...Qxd5 {And after the queen recaptures... We will play our d4 anyway.} 4.d4 4...Nf6 {Here, Black can ignore the pawn and develop. We'll just develop with Nf3. In Study 1, we saw a similar position where we played Nf3, but in that study ...e6 had been played. 5...cxd4 is a transposition to 4...cxd4, as seen in Study 1.} 5.Nf3 5...e6 {With 5...e6, Black wants to develop their dark-squared bishop. I like 6. Na3, intending Nb5.} 6.Na3 6...Nc6 {Black can develop and put pressure on d4. We'll threaten a fork on c7!} 7.Nb5 7...Bd6 {This move would be a terrible blunder. We'll play c4 and distract the queen from the defense of the bishop.} 8.c4 8...Qe4+ {This check is the only move. But after the simple block with Be2, the d6-bishop is still under attack.} 9.Be2 9...Bb8 {Once it goes back to b8, we can win a pawn and threaten a check on d6.} 10.dxc5 {This is winning for us. Black is down a pawn with no compensation. The bishop on b8 is also bad. That's not the proper square for it and the rooks won't be easily connected.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Lines (2...d5 and 2...Nf6) / Study 2: Black Plays 2...d5 and 4...Nf6 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/f9769969-d465-42af-80b0-4a8c5ed12f82/studies/08d50e70-dc88-4bd1-bbab-1e3276763aed/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. And we'll go for the Alapin.} 2.c3 2...d5 {Black breaks in the center with 2...d5. They don't want to suffer with a lack of space, so they expand in the center. Here we should capture.} 3.exd5 3...Qxd5 {And after the queen recaptures... We will play our d4 anyway.} 4.d4 4...Nf6 {Here, Black can ignore the pawn and develop. We'll just develop with Nf3. In Study 1, we saw a similar position where we played Nf3, but in that study ...e6 had been played. 5...cxd4 is a transposition to 4...cxd4, as seen in Study 1.} 5.Nf3 5...e6 {With 5...e6, Black wants to develop their dark-squared bishop. I like 6. Na3, intending Nb5.} 6.Na3 6...a6 {We'll play Nc4, and threaten a fork on b6.} 7.Nc4 7...Nbd7 {Black defends against it.} 8.a4 {Ensuring the knight can maintain its outpost on c4. We have seen this concept before.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Lines (2...d5 and 2...Nf6) / Study 2: Black Plays 2...d5 and 4...Nf6 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/f9769969-d465-42af-80b0-4a8c5ed12f82/studies/08d50e70-dc88-4bd1-bbab-1e3276763aed/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. And we'll go for the Alapin.} 2.c3 2...d5 {Black breaks in the center with 2...d5. They don't want to suffer with a lack of space, so they expand in the center. Here we should capture.} 3.exd5 3...Qxd5 {And after the queen recaptures... We will play our d4 anyway.} 4.d4 4...Nf6 {Here, Black can ignore the pawn and develop. We'll just develop with Nf3. In Study 1, we saw a similar position where we played Nf3, but in that study ...e6 had been played. 5...cxd4 is a transposition to 4...cxd4, as seen in Study 1.} 5.Nf3 5...Nc6 {5...Nc6 places the knight on a vulnerable square. This can go wrong quickly. King safety is first, so we'll play Be2. This blocks the e-file and prepares c4.} 6.Be2 6...Bf5 {If Black develops without ...e6, we can push. We'll play c4, getting ready to play d5.} 7.c4 7...Qd7 {The queen retreats to d7.} 8.d5 {But we can attack the knight right away. We'll likely see 8...Nb4. 9. Na3 is a good response, but the engine says that 9. 0-0 Nc2 is possible. The point is that we will have moves like Ne5 followed by g4 to attack the c2-knight's defender.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Lines (2...d5 and 2...Nf6) / Study 2: Black Plays 2...d5 and 4...Nf6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/f9769969-d465-42af-80b0-4a8c5ed12f82/studies/08d50e70-dc88-4bd1-bbab-1e3276763aed/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. And we'll go for the Alapin.} 2.c3 2...d5 {Black breaks in the center with 2...d5. They don't want to suffer with a lack of space, so they expand in the center. Here we should capture.} 3.exd5 3...Qxd5 {And after the queen recaptures... We will play our d4 anyway.} 4.d4 4...Nf6 {Here, Black can ignore the pawn and develop. We'll just develop with Nf3. In Study 1, we saw a similar position where we played Nf3, but in that study ...e6 had been played. 5...cxd4 is a transposition to 4...cxd4, as seen in Study 1.} 5.Nf3 5...e6 {With 5...e6, Black wants to develop their dark-squared bishop. I like 6. Na3, intending Nb5.} 6.Na3 6...Nc6 {Black can develop and put pressure on d4. We'll threaten a fork on c7!} 7.Nb5 7...Qd8 {Black is in some trouble here.} 8.dxc5 {We don't mind the queen trade because at the end the c7-square is still a problem for Black. Black's going to have to defend!} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Lines (2...d5 and 2...Nf6) / Study 2: Black Plays 2...d5 and 4...Nf6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/f9769969-d465-42af-80b0-4a8c5ed12f82/studies/08d50e70-dc88-4bd1-bbab-1e3276763aed/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. And we'll go for the Alapin.} 2.c3 2...d5 {Black breaks in the center with 2...d5. They don't want to suffer with a lack of space, so they expand in the center. Here we should capture.} 3.exd5 3...Qxd5 {And after the queen recaptures... We will play our d4 anyway.} 4.d4 4...Nf6 {Here, Black can ignore the pawn and develop. We'll just develop with Nf3. In Study 1, we saw a similar position where we played Nf3, but in that study ...e6 had been played. 5...cxd4 is a transposition to 4...cxd4, as seen in Study 1.} 5.Nf3 5...e6 {With 5...e6, Black wants to develop their dark-squared bishop. I like 6. Na3, intending Nb5.} 6.Na3 6...cxd4 {6...cxd4 allows us play, too. We'll bring the knight to b5 and threaten a fork on c7.} 7.Nb5 7...Qd8 {The queen defends. We'll propose a queen trade to remove the defender.} 8.Qxd4 8...Qxd4 {Black accepts. We'll recapture and have two really active knights.} 9.Nfxd4 9...Na6 {This is the best defense.} 10.Bf4 {But quite simply White is dominating with the active minor pieces aiming at the queenside. Nd6 will be a great move to play after Be2 and 0-0.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Lines (2...d5 and 2...Nf6) / Study 2: Black Plays 2...d5 and 4...Nf6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/f9769969-d465-42af-80b0-4a8c5ed12f82/studies/08d50e70-dc88-4bd1-bbab-1e3276763aed/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. And we'll go for the Alapin.} 2.c3 2...d5 {Black breaks in the center with 2...d5. They don't want to suffer with a lack of space, so they expand in the center. Here we should capture.} 3.exd5 3...Qxd5 {And after the queen recaptures... We will play our d4 anyway.} 4.d4 4...Nf6 {Here, Black can ignore the pawn and develop. We'll just develop with Nf3. In Study 1, we saw a similar position where we played Nf3, but in that study ...e6 had been played. 5...cxd4 is a transposition to 4...cxd4, as seen in Study 1.} 5.Nf3 5...Bg4 {Players love going for this pin: it's very popular. But Be2, gaining a tempo on the queen should minor pieces be exchanged on f3, is an effective solution.} 6.Be2 6...e6 {e6, to activate the f8-bishop, is often played. We'll castle.} 7.O-O 7...Nc6 {If pressure is applied on d4, we don't have to worry about the pawn just yet. 8. h3 is a useful move to insert. If Bxf3, we'll recapture and attack the queen and knight behind it.} 8.h3 8...Bh5 {That's why Black often doesn't capture. At this point I like defending the d4-pawn with Be3. cxd4 will allow us to recapture with the pawn and then put our knight on c3.} 9.Be3 9...Be7 {The bishop is developed to e7. The pawn capture on c5 leads to a good endgame for us, but it also sets up a trap. 10...Bxc5 loses the bishop to 11.Qxd5 and the bishop loses its defender.} 10.dxc5 10...Qxd1 {This trade of queens leads to an endgame in which Black needs to know what they're doing. White recaptures, with d-file control.} 11.Rxd1 11...Nd5 {Since our bishop defends c5, Black attacks it, trying to undermine the defense of c5. We'll play b4, holding on to the pawn.} 12.b4 12...Bf6 {Black targets c3, and also the b4-pawn, via the pin on the diagonal. This is why we'll push our b-pawn, seeing as it is being attacked anyway.} 13.b5 13...Ncb4 {When the knight goes to b4, we have a forcing line that sets up yet another opening trap for our opponents to deal with. Step one is to attack the bishop on h5.} 14.g4 14...Bg6 {The bishop is forced away. Now we'll attack Black's other bishop with g5! This is where Black could go wrong.} 15.g5 {Now we attack Black's other bishop! This is where Black could go wrong.} 15...Bxc3 {The correct move is 15...Bxc3. Nothing fancy, just going for the pawn. We'll just recapture with the knight.} 16.Nxc3 16...Nxc3 {Black will capture.} 17.Rd2 {And we'll defend our second rank. It will be important for us to play Ne5 and even Nc4 to maneuver. A trade with Nxe2+ is likely. That trade is of no concern: it gets rid of one of Black's most active pieces, and we can play our rook to b2 to attack the knight on b4.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Lines (2...d5 and 2...Nf6) / Study 2: Black Plays 2...d5 and 4...Nf6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/f9769969-d465-42af-80b0-4a8c5ed12f82/studies/08d50e70-dc88-4bd1-bbab-1e3276763aed/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. And we'll go for the Alapin.} 2.c3 2...d5 {Black breaks in the center with 2...d5. They don't want to suffer with a lack of space, so they expand in the center. Here we should capture.} 3.exd5 3...Qxd5 {And after the queen recaptures... We will play our d4 anyway.} 4.d4 4...Nf6 {Here, Black can ignore the pawn and develop. We'll just develop with Nf3. In Study 1, we saw a similar position where we played Nf3, but in that study ...e6 had been played. 5...cxd4 is a transposition to 4...cxd4, as seen in Study 1.} 5.Nf3 5...Bg4 {Players love going for this pin: it's very popular. But Be2, gaining a tempo on the queen should minor pieces be exchanged on f3, is an effective solution.} 6.Be2 6...e6 {e6, to activate the f8-bishop, is often played. We'll castle.} 7.O-O 7...Nc6 {If pressure is applied on d4, we don't have to worry about the pawn just yet. 8. h3 is a useful move to insert. If Bxf3, we'll recapture and attack the queen and knight behind it.} 8.h3 8...Bh5 {That's why Black often doesn't capture. At this point I like defending the d4-pawn with Be3. cxd4 will allow us to recapture with the pawn and then put our knight on c3.} 9.Be3 9...Be7 {The bishop is developed to e7. The pawn capture on c5 leads to a good endgame for us, but it also sets up a trap. 10...Bxc5 loses the bishop to 11.Qxd5 and the bishop loses its defender.} 10.dxc5 10...Qxd1 {This trade of queens leads to an endgame in which Black needs to know what they're doing. White recaptures, with d-file control.} 11.Rxd1 11...Nd5 {Since our bishop defends c5, Black attacks it, trying to undermine the defense of c5. We'll play b4, holding on to the pawn.} 12.b4 12...Bf6 {Black targets c3, and also the b4-pawn, via the pin on the diagonal. This is why we'll push our b-pawn, seeing as it is being attacked anyway.} 13.b5 13...Ncb4 {When the knight goes to b4, we have a forcing line that sets up yet another opening trap for our opponents to deal with. Step one is to attack the bishop on h5.} 14.g4 14...Bg6 {The bishop is forced away. Now we'll attack Black's other bishop with g5! This is where Black could go wrong.} 15.g5 {Now we attack Black's other bishop! This is where Black could go wrong.} 15...Nxc3 {This is the little trap we set up. It looks like Black has a lot of activity. But it all becomes illusory once we take the bishop.} 16.gxf6 16...Nxe2+ {Even though this move is check, the knight will come under attack. We'll play Kf1. Now both the knight and the g-pawn are en prise.} 17.Kf1 17...Nc2 {Sure, Black can go after the rook in the corner.} 18.Kxe2 {But that would only win an exchange, not a full rook. We will take on g7 and play the strong Bg5 followed by Bf6. White is winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Lines (2...d5 and 2...Nf6) / Study 3: Black Plays 2...Nf6, 5...e6 and 5...Nc6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/f9769969-d465-42af-80b0-4a8c5ed12f82/studies/35948b18-65ba-4a8e-ba41-76f370196b90/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll play c3 and go for the Alapin.} 2.c3 2...Nf6 {The move 2...Nf6 is the most popular move in the games of titled players. However, under 2000 it is played in only 8% of games. Now e5 is our most challenging continuation. We attack the knight.} 3.e5 3...Nd5 {3...Ne4 gets the knight trapped with 4. d3, so Nd5 is best. But now we can strengthen the center. We'll play d4 and connect our pawns.} 4.d4 4...cxd4 {Black takes on d4. We won't recapture back right away. The d-pawn is pinned, so we'll develop a piece with Nf3.} 5.Nf3 5...Nc6 {5...Nc6 is a logical move to develop a piece and pressure the center. Attacking the knight with the bishop is a great idea. We don't care about recapturing on d4 right away.} 6.Bc4 6...Nb6 {The option you'll see the most is probably Nb6 to attack your bishop. We should just put it on b3. Black now has three options. 7...d5 and 7...d6 are essentially the same because you would take on d6 in both scenarios. We'll consider them in Study 4.} 7.Bb3 7...dxc3 {The most obvious is to grab the pawn. We can recapture with our knight and develop.} 8.Nxc3 8...d6 {Black could undermine our e5-pawn. It's possible to ignore e5 and just castle. There are tactics in the center.} 9.O-O 9...dxe5 {Black should not take this, although I am sure many people will! The queen trade is winning. If the king takes the queen, f7 can be attacked with Ng5.} 10.Qxd8+ 10...Nxd8 {If the knight takes, e5 isn't defended, but we're not going after the pawn. We'll play Nb5. We're going for the rook!} 11.Nb5 11...Ne6 {11...Ne6 defends c7 for one move. But we can remove the defender.} 12.Bxe6 12...Bxe6 {The bishop recaptures. We'll give a check, forking the king and rook.} 13.Nc7+ 13...Kd8 {The king goes to d8. We'll take the rook.} 14.Nxa8 14...Nxa8 {Black recaptures. We'll go after the bishop which is Black's only developed piece.} 15.Ng5 15...Bc4 {Black can avoid the trade.} 16.Rd1+ {But now it's only a matter of technique. If you know your Alapin well, this is the kind of win you can get!} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Lines (2...d5 and 2...Nf6) / Study 3: Black Plays 2...Nf6, 5...e6 and 5...Nc6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/f9769969-d465-42af-80b0-4a8c5ed12f82/studies/35948b18-65ba-4a8e-ba41-76f370196b90/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll play c3 and go for the Alapin.} 2.c3 2...Nf6 {The move 2...Nf6 is the most popular move in the games of titled players. However, under 2000 it is played in only 8% of games. Now e5 is our most challenging continuation. We attack the knight.} 3.e5 3...Nd5 {3...Ne4 gets the knight trapped with 4. d3, so Nd5 is best. But now we can strengthen the center. We'll play d4 and connect our pawns.} 4.d4 4...cxd4 {Black takes on d4. We won't recapture back right away. The d-pawn is pinned, so we'll develop a piece with Nf3.} 5.Nf3 5...Nc6 {5...Nc6 is a logical move to develop a piece and pressure the center. Attacking the knight with the bishop is a great idea. We don't care about recapturing on d4 right away.} 6.Bc4 6...e6 {Some players could defend the knight with the pawn, thinking that they're fine with the trade because they get the bishop pair. We can capture on d5.} 7.Bxd5 7...exd5 {Black recaptures. And we can castle kingside.} 8.O-O 8...Be7 {Black could continue with simple development. We should develop and defend e5. If Black instead plays 8...dxc3, that helps us develop with 9.Nxc3.} 9.Bf4 9...O-O {Black castles. And we can capture, centralizing our knight. It's also a blockader of those doubled d-pawns.} 10.Nxd4 10...d6 {Black should probably play d6. The idea is to get rid of the doubled pawns and lessen our central control. We have to take.} 11.exd6 11...Bxd6 {Black recaptures. And since trading pieces is good when playing against the IQP, we shouldn't mind trading bishops.} 12.Bxd6 12...Qxd6 {Once the queen recaptures, we can develop our other knight.} 13.Na3 {We're going to play a middlegame following the classic principles of playing against the IQP. We want to blockade the pawn, trade pieces whenever possible (but not Nxc6 because we fix Black's structure).} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Lines (2...d5 and 2...Nf6) / Study 3: Black Plays 2...Nf6, 5...e6 and 5...Nc6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/f9769969-d465-42af-80b0-4a8c5ed12f82/studies/35948b18-65ba-4a8e-ba41-76f370196b90/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll play c3 and go for the Alapin.} 2.c3 2...Nf6 {The move 2...Nf6 is the most popular move in the games of titled players. However, under 2000 it is played in only 8% of games. Now e5 is our most challenging continuation. We attack the knight.} 3.e5 3...Nd5 {3...Ne4 gets the knight trapped with 4. d3, so Nd5 is best. But now we can strengthen the center. We'll play d4 and connect our pawns.} 4.d4 4...cxd4 {Black takes on d4. We won't recapture back right away. The d-pawn is pinned, so we'll develop a piece with Nf3.} 5.Nf3 5...Nc6 {5...Nc6 is a logical move to develop a piece and pressure the center. Attacking the knight with the bishop is a great idea. We don't care about recapturing on d4 right away.} 6.Bc4 6...Nb6 {The option you'll see the most is probably Nb6 to attack your bishop. We should just put it on b3. Black now has three options. 7...d5 and 7...d6 are essentially the same because you would take on d6 in both scenarios. We'll consider them in Study 4.} 7.Bb3 7...dxc3 {The most obvious is to grab the pawn. We can recapture with our knight and develop.} 8.Nxc3 8...e6 {There's one major drawback to ...e6. The d6-square is weakened. If we reinforce our central pawn, we make it harder for Black to play ...d6.} 9.Bf4 9...Bb4 {The bishop can pin our knight, but this isn't optimal development in the Alapin. We can just castle because we are not troubled by ...Bxc3.} 10.O-O 10...O-O {Black castles as well.} 11.Bg3 {And we can place our bishop on a safer square. I really like the idea of getting a knight to d6. It will be a monster on that square. The only thing that could alter our plan is ...Bxc3, but even then we have other ideas. Bc2 to target h7 for example.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Lines (2...d5 and 2...Nf6) / Study 3: Black Plays 2...Nf6, 5...e6 and 5...Nc6 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/f9769969-d465-42af-80b0-4a8c5ed12f82/studies/35948b18-65ba-4a8e-ba41-76f370196b90/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll play c3 and go for the Alapin.} 2.c3 2...Nf6 {The move 2...Nf6 is the most popular move in the games of titled players. However, under 2000 it is played in only 8% of games. Now e5 is our most challenging continuation. We attack the knight.} 3.e5 3...Nd5 {3...Ne4 gets the knight trapped with 4. d3, so Nd5 is best. But now we can strengthen the center. We'll play d4 and connect our pawns.} 4.d4 4...cxd4 {Black takes on d4. We won't recapture back right away. The d-pawn is pinned, so we'll develop a piece with Nf3.} 5.Nf3 5...e6 {Black could also choose to support the knight in the center. We'll develop and start applying some pressure to the knight.} 6.Bc4 6...Nb6 {A very natural reaction is to play the knight to b6 and attack the bishop. This will allow Black to go up a pawn. We'll retreat the bishop, accepting that Black can take on c3. We don't mind because we will have a lead in development.} 7.Bb3 7...dxc3 {Black takes. And we recapture.} 8.Nxc3 8...d5 {Let's focus on 8...d5. This is the correct move and it isn't too hard to find. Our approach will be to capture en passant to break apart Black's central formation. We don't want to allow them a strong pawn chain. } 9.exd6 9...Bxd6 {Black recaptures with the bishop. But we have quick development. Since there's no tension between the queens, we can castle. We don't want a trade of queens since we want activity.} 10.O-O 10...O-O {Black castles as well. And now it's time to make use of our d-file. We'll shift the queen to e2, in preparation for Rd1.} 11.Qe2 11...Nc6 {Black could continue by developing.} 12.Rd1 {And we can pin the bishop on d6. ...Qc7 is a trivial mistake because you get Nb5, a fork. So you'll probably see 12...Qe7. A nice plan is to go Bg5 to induce the f6 weakness and bring the bishop to e3. You have good compensation for the pawn!} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Lines (2...d5 and 2...Nf6) / Study 3: Black Plays 2...Nf6, 5...e6 and 5...Nc6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/f9769969-d465-42af-80b0-4a8c5ed12f82/studies/35948b18-65ba-4a8e-ba41-76f370196b90/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll play c3 and go for the Alapin.} 2.c3 2...Nf6 {The move 2...Nf6 is the most popular move in the games of titled players. However, under 2000 it is played in only 8% of games. Now e5 is our most challenging continuation. We attack the knight.} 3.e5 3...Nd5 {3...Ne4 gets the knight trapped with 4. d3, so Nd5 is best. But now we can strengthen the center. We'll play d4 and connect our pawns.} 4.d4 4...cxd4 {Black takes on d4. We won't recapture back right away. The d-pawn is pinned, so we'll develop a piece with Nf3.} 5.Nf3 5...Nc6 {5...Nc6 is a logical move to develop a piece and pressure the center. Attacking the knight with the bishop is a great idea. We don't care about recapturing on d4 right away.} 6.Bc4 6...e6 {Some players could defend the knight with the pawn, thinking that they're fine with the trade because they get the bishop pair. We can capture on d5.} 7.Bxd5 7...exd5 {Black recaptures. And we can castle kingside.} 8.O-O 8...d6 {Black could also try undermining our e5-pawn. We should defend e5.} 9.Bf4 9...dxe5 {Black takes. And we'll recapture with the bishop so that if there's a further trade we end up with a knight on e5.} 10.Bxe5 10...Nxe5 {Black takes. And we'll recapture.} 11.Nxe5 11...Bd6 {The bishop goes to d6 because Black wants to castle soon. But since our knight is on e5, Qa4+ is quite effective.} 12.Qa4+ 12...Bd7 {We're inducing the bishop to d7 and we can trade it off. We'll take on d7.} 13.Nxd7 13...Qxd7 {The queen recaptures. Now Re1 forces Black into a self-pin.} 14.Re1+ 14...Be7 {Black has to block it with the bishop.} 15.Qxd4 {But now material is equal and we have some activity. We want to play Nd2 to f3 and Re1 to double up rooks on the e-file.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Lines (2...d5 and 2...Nf6) / Study 3: Black Plays 2...Nf6, 5...e6 and 5...Nc6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/f9769969-d465-42af-80b0-4a8c5ed12f82/studies/35948b18-65ba-4a8e-ba41-76f370196b90/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll play c3 and go for the Alapin.} 2.c3 2...Nf6 {The move 2...Nf6 is the most popular move in the games of titled players. However, under 2000 it is played in only 8% of games. Now e5 is our most challenging continuation. We attack the knight.} 3.e5 3...Nd5 {3...Ne4 gets the knight trapped with 4. d3, so Nd5 is best. But now we can strengthen the center. We'll play d4 and connect our pawns.} 4.d4 4...cxd4 {Black takes on d4. We won't recapture back right away. The d-pawn is pinned, so we'll develop a piece with Nf3.} 5.Nf3 5...Nc6 {5...Nc6 is a logical move to develop a piece and pressure the center. Attacking the knight with the bishop is a great idea. We don't care about recapturing on d4 right away.} 6.Bc4 6...Nb6 {The option you'll see the most is probably Nb6 to attack your bishop. We should just put it on b3. Black now has three options. 7...d5 and 7...d6 are essentially the same because you would take on d6 in both scenarios. We'll consider them in Study 4.} 7.Bb3 7...e6 {7...e6 by itself causes no harm to Black. As long as Black plays ...d6 or ...d5 later on, this move is fine. We can take on d4 and strengthen the pawn on e5.} 8.cxd4 8...d6 {After 8...d6 to undermine our structure, we want to avoid queen trades. And we want to support the e5-pawn, so we'll play Qe2.} 9.Qe2 9...dxe5 {This trade can happen, and it probably should for Black's sake.} 10.dxe5 {We can just recapture. We want to castle and play a rook to our c- and d-files. The bishop's safest square for now would probably be d2. We have a slight edge thanks to the e5-pawn!} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Lines (2...d5 and 2...Nf6) / Study 3: Black Plays 2...Nf6, 5...e6 and 5...Nc6 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/f9769969-d465-42af-80b0-4a8c5ed12f82/studies/35948b18-65ba-4a8e-ba41-76f370196b90/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll play c3 and go for the Alapin.} 2.c3 2...Nf6 {The move 2...Nf6 is the most popular move in the games of titled players. However, under 2000 it is played in only 8% of games. Now e5 is our most challenging continuation. We attack the knight.} 3.e5 3...Nd5 {3...Ne4 gets the knight trapped with 4. d3, so Nd5 is best. But now we can strengthen the center. We'll play d4 and connect our pawns.} 4.d4 4...cxd4 {Black takes on d4. We won't recapture back right away. The d-pawn is pinned, so we'll develop a piece with Nf3.} 5.Nf3 5...e6 {Black could also choose to support the knight in the center. We'll develop and start applying some pressure to the knight.} 6.Bc4 6...d6 {Black could choose to undermine the e-pawn. This allows us the trade on d5. This is a good idea because it wrecks Black's structure.} 7.Bxd5 7...exd5 {When they recapture, they temporarily have tripled pawns. Of course, they'll solve this issue soon. We should support the e5-pawn with the bishop because our pieces will be active in the center.} 8.Bf4 8...dxe5 {If Black takes, we can just recapture.} 9.Bxe5 {We'll castle soon. If 9...dxc3, 10. Nxc3 will give us three developed minor pieces to Black's zero. We should have a nice middlegame due to our activity, with pressure on the central d5-pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Lines (2...d5 and 2...Nf6) / Study 4: Black Plays 2...Nf6, 5...Nc6 and 7...d5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/f9769969-d465-42af-80b0-4a8c5ed12f82/studies/6b3372cf-c341-4b54-828a-a6d5abe6174a/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll play c3 and go for the Alapin.} 2.c3 2...Nf6 {The move 2...Nf6 is the most popular move in the games of titled players. However, under 2000 it is played in only 8% of games. Now e5 is the most challenging. We attack the knight.} 3.e5 3...Nd5 {3...Ne4 gets the knight trapped with 4. d3, so Nd5 is the best. But now we can strengthen the center. We'll play d4 and connect our pawns.} 4.d4 4...cxd4 {Black takes on d4. And we won't recapture back right away. The d-pawn is pinned, so we'll develop a piece with Nf3.} 5.Nf3 5...Nc6 {5...Nc6 is a logical move to develop a piece and pressure the center. Attacking the knight with Bc4 is a great idea. We don't care about recapturing on d4 right away.} 6.Bc4 6...Nb6 {The option you'll see the most is probably Nb6 to attack the bishop. We should just retreat it to b3.} 7.Bb3 7...d5 {...d5 and ...d6 are now equivalent moves, in the sense that we will capture the pawn either way. After d5, we will take en passant to get rid of the strong d-pawn. Our ideas still have to do with quick development.} 8.exd6 8...Qxd6 {The queen recaptures. Castling is our main idea. We do permit ...dxc3, but this shouldn't be an issue thanks to our development lead after Nxc3.} 9.O-O 9...Bg4 {This move is a pin on our knight, but we have a clever tactic. We'll capture on f7 with check!} 10.Bxf7+ 10...Kxf7 {The king takes. We give a check. This is also a discovered attack on the g4-bishop.} 11.Ng5+ 11...Kg8 {The king retreats.} 12.Qxg4 {And we recover the piece with some interest: Black's coordination is terrible. The king on g8 is vulnerable and prevents the rooks from being connected. If 12...e5, 13. f4 is good. White is considerably better.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Lines (2...d5 and 2...Nf6) / Study 4: Black Plays 2...Nf6, 5...Nc6 and 7...d5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/f9769969-d465-42af-80b0-4a8c5ed12f82/studies/6b3372cf-c341-4b54-828a-a6d5abe6174a/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll play c3 and go for the Alapin.} 2.c3 2...Nf6 {The move 2...Nf6 is the most popular move in the games of titled players. However, under 2000 it is played in only 8% of games. Now e5 is the most challenging. We attack the knight.} 3.e5 3...Nd5 {3...Ne4 gets the knight trapped with 4. d3, so Nd5 is the best. But now we can strengthen the center. We'll play d4 and connect our pawns.} 4.d4 4...cxd4 {Black takes on d4. And we won't recapture back right away. The d-pawn is pinned, so we'll develop a piece with Nf3.} 5.Nf3 5...Nc6 {5...Nc6 is a logical move to develop a piece and pressure the center. Attacking the knight with Bc4 is a great idea. We don't care about recapturing on d4 right away.} 6.Bc4 6...Nb6 {The option you'll see the most is probably Nb6 to attack the bishop. We should just retreat it to b3.} 7.Bb3 7...d5 {...d5 and ...d6 are now equivalent moves, in the sense that we will capture the pawn either way. After d5, we will take en passant to get rid of the strong d-pawn. Our ideas still have to do with quick development.} 8.exd6 8...Qxd6 {The queen recaptures. Castling is our main idea. We do permit ...dxc3, but this shouldn't be an issue thanks to our development lead after Nxc3.} 9.O-O 9...Be6 {Black may offer a trade of bishops. Our bishop is more active on b3, so this makes sense. Bringing our knight out via a3, intending to go to b5, is a dynamic way to play this.} 10.Na3 10...dxc3 {If this capture happens now, we will avoid the queen trade. We'll go to e2 and prepare Rd1.} 11.Qe2 11...Bxb3 {Black could go for this trade. We have an interesting in-between move. The knight on b5 is powerful. It aims at the c7-square for the fork.} 12.Nb5 12...Qb8 {Since the queen isn't safe on d8, it must go to b8.} 13.axb3 {But b8 isn't the best of squares for the queen. We can recapture and open up the a-file for our rook. We'll continue with Bg5 and Re1 to put pressure on the e-file.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Lines (2...d5 and 2...Nf6) / Study 4: Black Plays 2...Nf6, 5...Nc6 and 7...d5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/f9769969-d465-42af-80b0-4a8c5ed12f82/studies/6b3372cf-c341-4b54-828a-a6d5abe6174a/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll play c3 and go for the Alapin.} 2.c3 2...Nf6 {The move 2...Nf6 is the most popular move in the games of titled players. However, under 2000 it is played in only 8% of games. Now e5 is the most challenging. We attack the knight.} 3.e5 3...Nd5 {3...Ne4 gets the knight trapped with 4. d3, so Nd5 is the best. But now we can strengthen the center. We'll play d4 and connect our pawns.} 4.d4 4...cxd4 {Black takes on d4. And we won't recapture back right away. The d-pawn is pinned, so we'll develop a piece with Nf3.} 5.Nf3 5...Nc6 {5...Nc6 is a logical move to develop a piece and pressure the center. Attacking the knight with Bc4 is a great idea. We don't care about recapturing on d4 right away.} 6.Bc4 6...Nb6 {The option you'll see the most is probably Nb6 to attack the bishop. We should just retreat it to b3.} 7.Bb3 7...d5 {...d5 and ...d6 are now equivalent moves, in the sense that we will capture the pawn either way. After d5, we will take en passant to get rid of the strong d-pawn. Our ideas still have to do with quick development.} 8.exd6 8...Qxd6 {The queen recaptures. Castling is our main idea. We do permit ...dxc3, but this shouldn't be an issue thanks to our development lead after Nxc3.} 9.O-O 9...Be6 {Black may offer a trade of bishops. Our bishop is more active on b3, so this makes sense. Bringing our knight out via a3, intending to go to b5, is a dynamic way to play this.} 10.Na3 10...dxc3 {If this capture happens now, we will avoid the queen trade. We'll go to e2 and prepare Rd1.} 11.Qe2 11...cxb2 {Black could be greedy and take another pawn. They're giving us a big lead in development.} 12.Bxb2 {We have a lot of compensation. It won't be easy for Black to hold on to the material. We will play Rad1 and Rfe1 to fully maximize our pressure, and Nb5 if Black allows it.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Lines (2...d5 and 2...Nf6) / Study 4: Black Plays 2...Nf6, 5...Nc6 and 7...d5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/f9769969-d465-42af-80b0-4a8c5ed12f82/studies/6b3372cf-c341-4b54-828a-a6d5abe6174a/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll play c3 and go for the Alapin.} 2.c3 2...Nf6 {The move 2...Nf6 is the most popular move in the games of titled players. However, under 2000 it is played in only 8% of games. Now e5 is the most challenging. We attack the knight.} 3.e5 3...Nd5 {3...Ne4 gets the knight trapped with 4. d3, so Nd5 is the best. But now we can strengthen the center. We'll play d4 and connect our pawns.} 4.d4 4...cxd4 {Black takes on d4. And we won't recapture back right away. The d-pawn is pinned, so we'll develop a piece with Nf3.} 5.Nf3 5...Nc6 {5...Nc6 is a logical move to develop a piece and pressure the center. Attacking the knight with Bc4 is a great idea. We don't care about recapturing on d4 right away.} 6.Bc4 6...Nb6 {The option you'll see the most is probably Nb6 to attack the bishop. We should just retreat it to b3.} 7.Bb3 7...d5 {...d5 and ...d6 are now equivalent moves, in the sense that we will capture the pawn either way. After d5, we will take en passant to get rid of the strong d-pawn. Our ideas still have to do with quick development.} 8.exd6 8...Qxd6 {The queen recaptures. Castling is our main idea. We do permit ...dxc3, but this shouldn't be an issue thanks to our development lead after Nxc3.} 9.O-O 9...Be6 {Black may offer a trade of bishops. Our bishop is more active on b3, so this makes sense. Bringing our knight out via a3, intending to go to b5, is a dynamic way to play this.} 10.Na3 10...Bxb3 {Black could also take on b3. When we recapture with the queen, we activate it and aim at f7. We also eye b7 indirectly and clear the d1-square for our rook.} 11.Qxb3 11...dxc3 {Black could take on c3, but this allows us to play Nb5 with tempo. The black queen now has to stay and guard c7.} 12.Nb5 12...Qd7 {If something like 12...Qb8, Ng5 attacks f7. But this move has drawbacks too. We'll bring our rook to the d-file with tempo.} 13.Rd1 13...Qc8 {The queen moves away.} 14.Bf4 {And we're forcing our way to the c7-square. All thanks to offering material in exchange for development.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Lines (2...d5 and 2...Nf6) / Study 4: Black Plays 2...Nf6, 5...Nc6 and 7...d5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/3af40081-374e-4a1d-bc24-eaab86295502/chapters/f9769969-d465-42af-80b0-4a8c5ed12f82/studies/6b3372cf-c341-4b54-828a-a6d5abe6174a/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Sicilian. We'll play c3 and go for the Alapin.} 2.c3 2...Nf6 {The move 2...Nf6 is the most popular move in the games of titled players. However, under 2000 it is played in only 8% of games. Now e5 is the most challenging. We attack the knight.} 3.e5 3...Nd5 {3...Ne4 gets the knight trapped with 4. d3, so Nd5 is the best. But now we can strengthen the center. We'll play d4 and connect our pawns.} 4.d4 4...cxd4 {Black takes on d4. And we won't recapture back right away. The d-pawn is pinned, so we'll develop a piece with Nf3.} 5.Nf3 5...Nc6 {5...Nc6 is a logical move to develop a piece and pressure the center. Attacking the knight with Bc4 is a great idea. We don't care about recapturing on d4 right away.} 6.Bc4 6...Nb6 {The option you'll see the most is probably Nb6 to attack the bishop. We should just retreat it to b3.} 7.Bb3 7...d5 {...d5 and ...d6 are now equivalent moves, in the sense that we will capture the pawn either way. After d5, we will take en passant to get rid of the strong d-pawn. Our ideas still have to do with quick development.} 8.exd6 8...Qxd6 {The queen recaptures. Castling is our main idea. We do permit ...dxc3, but this shouldn't be an issue thanks to our development lead after Nxc3.} 9.O-O 9...e6 {9...e6 serves to enable the development of the f8-bishop.} 10.cxd4 {We should just capture and get ready to play Bg5. All our pieces are coming out and if Black isn't careful we'll achieve the d5-pawn push. Otherwise, we'll just play a standard IQP position, avoiding piece trades and getting a knight to e5.} *